
04. LONGEST CONSECUTIVE SEQUENCE

1. Problem Statement

Given an unsorted array, find the length of the longest sequence of consecutive values. The values do not need to be adjacent in the original array.

Example:
Input: [100, 4, 200, 1, 3, 2]
Output: 4

The longest sequence is [1, 2, 3, 4].

2. Brute-Force Approach

For every element, repeatedly search the array for the next consecutive value.

Complexity:
Time: O(n^2)
Auxiliary Space: O(1)

3. Approach B: Sorting

Sort the array and scan it, ignoring duplicates and counting consecutive values.

Complexity:
Time: O(n log n)
Auxiliary Space: Typically O(log n) for the sorting stack.

4. Optimized Approach: Hash Set

Store all values in an unordered_set. Start counting only when the current value has no predecessor in the set.

Code:

#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <unordered_set>
#include <algorithm>
#include <climits>
using namespace std;

int longestConsecutive(vector<int>& a) {
    unordered_set<int> values(a.begin(), a.end());
    int best = 0;

    for (int x : values) {
        if (x != INT_MIN && values.count(x - 1)) {
            continue;
        }

        int current = x;
        int length = 1;

        while (current != INT_MAX &&
               values.count(current + 1)) {
            current++;
            length++;
        }

        best = max(best, length);
    }

    return best;
}

int main() {
    vector<int> a = {100, 4, 200, 1, 3, 2};

    cout << longestConsecutive(a);

    return 0;
}

5. Complexity

Expected Time: O(n)
Auxiliary Space: O(n)

Worst-case time with pathological hash collisions: O(n^2).

6. Edge Cases

[] -> 0
[5] -> 1
[1, 2, 2, 3] -> 3
[9, 1, 4, 7, 3, 2, 6, 8] -> 8
